w-up"}]]}],["$","tr","4",{"className":"even:bg-gray-50/50","children":[["$","td","0",{"className":"px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 border-b border-gray-100 align-top leading-relaxed","children":"Specimen type and site"}],["$","td","1",{"className":"px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 border-b border-gray-100 align-top leading-relaxed","children":"Where relevant — which site, which side"}]]}]]}]]}]}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"text-sm text-gray-500 mt-2 italic","children":"Specimen labelling requirements"}]]}]
18:["$","div","2",{"className":"p-4 rounded-xl border-l-4 text-gray-800 leading-relaxed bg-yellow-50 border-yellow-400","children":[false,"Label in the presence of the patient, immediately after collection, before leaving the room. Never pre-label containers before collection, and never carry unlabelled specimens to a desk to label later. Both practices are how specimens are switched, and a mislabelled specimen means a patient is treated on someone else's results."]}]
19:["$","p","3",{"className":"text-gray-700 leading-relaxed","children":"CLIA — the Clinical Laboratory Improvement Amendments — regulates all laboratory testing on human specimens in the United States. Every site performing testing needs a CLIA certificate, and tests are categorized by complexity: waived, moderate, and high."}]
1a:["$","figure","4",{"children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-gray-200","children":["$","table",null,{"className":"w-full text-left border-collapse","children":[["$","thead",null,{"children":["$","tr",null,{"className":"bg-gray-50","children":[["$","th","0",{"scope":"col","className":"px-4 py-3 text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200","children":"Aspect"}],["$","th","1",{"scope":"col","className":"px-4 py-3 text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200","children":"Detail"}]]}]}],["$","tbody",null,{"children":[["$","tr","0",{"className":"even:bg-gray-50/50","children":[["$","td","0",{"className":"px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 border-b border-gray-100 align-top leading-relaxed","children":"What it is"}],["$","td","1",{"className":"px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 border-b border-gray-100 align-top leading-relaxed","children":"Tests simple enough that the risk of erroneous results is negligible"}]]}],["$","tr","1",{"className":"even:bg-gray-50/50","children":[["$","td","0",{"className":"px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 border-b border-gray-100 align-top leading-relaxed","children":"Examples"}],["$","td","1",{"className":"px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 border-b border-gray-100 align-top leading-relaxed","children":"Urine dipstick, rapid strep, blood glucose meters, rapid influenza"}]]}],["$","tr","2",{"className":"even:bg-gray-50/50","children":[["$","td","0",{"className":"px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 border-b border-gray-100 align-top leading-relaxed","children":"Who may perform"}],["$","td","1",{"className":"px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 border-b border-gray-100 align-top leading-relaxed","children":"Trained unlicensed personnel, including MAs, under the practice's certificate"}]]}],["$","tr","3",{"className":"even:bg-gray-50/50","children":[["$","td","0",{"className":"px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 border-b border-gray-100 align-top leading-relaxed","children":"Still required"}],["$","td","1",{"className":"px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 border-b border-gray-100 align-top leading-relaxed","children":"Follow the manufacturer's instructions exactly; run quality controls; document"}]]}]]}]]}]}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"text-sm text-gray-500 mt-2 italic","children":"CLIA-waived testing"}]]}]
1b:["$","div","5",{"className":"p-4 rounded-xl border-l-4 text-gray-800 leading-relaxed bg-blue-50 border-blue-400","children":[false,"Waived does not mean casual. A test loses its waived status the moment you deviate from the manufacturer's instructions — using an expired cartridge, altering an incubation time, or reading a result outside its valid window. At that point the practice is performing non-waived testing without authorization, which is a regulatory violation as well as a clinical risk."]}]
1c:["$","p","6",{"className":"text-gray-700 leading-relaxed","children":"Quality control confirms the test system is working before patient results are trusted. Run controls at the frequency the manufacturer and your policy require, record the results, and if a control fails, stop testing patients on that system until it is resolved. A failed control that is repeated until it passes is not a resolution."}]
1d:["$","$L21","7",{"block":{"type":"knowledge-check","question":"A rapid strep test kit's control fails. What should you do?","options":["Run the patient test anyway and note the control failure","Stop using that kit or lot for patient testing until the failure is resolved, and follow the practice's QC procedure","Repeat the control until it passes","Use a different kit and discard the failed control result"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"A failed control means the test system cannot be relied on. Patient results from it are not trustworthy, so testing stops until the cause is identified and resolved. Repeating until a control passes discards the information the failure was giving you.","optionRationales":["A result from an unreliable system is worse than no result, because it will be acted upon.","Correct. Stop patient testing, investigate, and document per the QC procedure.","Repeating until it passes treats the control as an obstacle rather than a check, and hides a real problem.","Switching kits without investigating and documenting the failure skips the required QC process."]}}]
1e:["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-12","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-8","children":[["$","h2",null,{"className":"text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3","children":"Challenge"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed max-w-3xl","children":"Apply what you've learned in this lesson."}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"bg-white rounded-2xl shadow-md border border-gray-200 p-8","children":["$","div",null,{"className":"space-y-6","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-gray-700 leading-relaxed","children":"Understand the regulations governing what your practice may test."}],["$","ol","1",{"start":"$undefined","className":"list-decimal pl-6 space-y-3 text-gray-700 marker:font-semibold marker:text-primary-600","children":[["$","li","0",{"className":"leading-relaxed pl-1","children":"Find the CMS list of CLIA-waived tests. Identify five commonly performed in a family practice."}],["$","li","1",{"className":"leading-relaxed pl-1","children":"For a rapid strep test you research, list every manufacturer requirement — storage, expiration, incubation time, reading window."}],["$","li","2",{"className":"leading-relaxed pl-1","children":"Write a specimen labelling procedure that makes a mix-up structurally difficult rather than merely discouraged."}],["$","li","3",{"className":"leading-relaxed pl-1","children":"Research chain of custody. Explain in three sentences when it is required and what it demands."}]]}]]}]}]]}]
